# The one place the deployment targets are written down.
#
# Change an address here and every script, both boards and the wall panel follow.
# Nothing else in this repo should contain a board IP address: if you find
# yourself typing one into a script, add it here instead.
#
# Why this file exists: on 2026-09-12 the Orange Pi moved from Wi-Fi to Ethernet
# and its address changed. Thirty-four files had to be edited, the wall panel sat
# on a dead URL, two Matter services ran `active` while bound to an interface
# that no longer existed, and every camera on the dashboard broke. One address,
# written down once, would have been one edit.
#
# These are plain `KEY=value` lines - no quoting, no expansion, no comments after
# a value - because both a shell and a Python parser have to agree on them.
#
# Everything here can still be overridden per-invocation, and that wins:
#   PI_HOST=192.168.0.99 ./scripts/deploy-dashboard.sh
#   ./scripts/deploy-to-pi.sh --host 192.168.0.99

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DHCP reservations - do this, or the addresses below will move on their own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both addresses are DHCP leases from the router at 192.168.0.1 (D-Link
# DIR-859), and the observed lease time is **7200 s - two hours**. That is short
# enough that a router reboot or a machine being off for an afternoon can hand
# the address to something else. Neither box asks for a specific address.
#
# Reserve both, keyed on MAC, in the router's admin UI at http://192.168.0.1
# (Settings -> Network -> DHCP Server -> DHCP Reservation on this model):
#
#   192.168.0.83   c0:74:2b:f8:d1:c4   orangepi6plus  enp97s0  (Ethernet, in use)
#   192.168.0.176  e4:5f:01:71:0f:2a   smarthome      wlan0    (wall panel, Wi-Fi)
#
# Two more MACs worth reserving to the same addresses if those links are ever
# used, so moving a cable does not move an address:
#
#   c0:74:2b:f8:d1:c5   orangepi6plus  enp49s0  (the board's second 2.5G port)
#   e4:5f:01:71:0f:29   smarthome      eth0     (the panel, if it is ever wired)
#
# The board's Wi-Fi (wlp1s0) was disconnected on 2026-09-12 and the interface no
# longer exists, so it has no MAC to reserve.
#
# Verify a reservation took, from this machine:
#
#   ssh orangepi@192.168.0.83 'nmcli -t -f DHCP4 device show enp97s0 | grep ip_address'
#
# and confirm it survives by rebooting the board, or by releasing the lease:
#
#   ssh orangepi@192.168.0.83 'sudo nmcli device reapply enp97s0'
#
# Why this is not optional. The panel's address is a *credential*: it logs
# itself into both the dashboard and Home Assistant by IP, so if DHCP moves it
# the panel is locked out, and if DHCP hands that address to another device,
# that device inherits the panel's access. See docs/kiosk-display.md. The
# board's address is what the panel, every deploy script and this file point at.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Orange Pi 6 Plus - the primary target, and the machine that runs the house.
# Ethernet (enp97s0) since 2026-09-12. Reserve it - see above.
PI_HOST=192.168.0.83
PI_USER=orangepi
REMOTE_PATH=/home/orangepi/smart_home_AI

# Raspberry Pi 4 - secondary build target, and the wall panel since 2026-09-10.
# Its address is a credential, which is why the reservation above matters more
# here than anywhere else. See docs/kiosk-display.md before changing it.
RPI4_HOST=192.168.0.176
RPI4_USER=smarthome
RPI4_REMOTE_PATH=/home/smarthome/smart-home-rpi4

# The dashboard the wall panel displays, and where go2rtc answers. Cameras do
# not need an address at all - `go2rtc_url: auto` in devices.local.yaml resolves
# to whatever the board's address currently is.
DASHBOARD_PORT=8000
